From 5a300bef9f5f3d022a0a4a0cf23a70767b6c7000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lumnus <lumnus.org@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 12:04:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0018-b70-offload-chunked-pinned-pool: kv_offload: split the
 pinned CPU KV pool when one allocation is too large

The CPU offloading worker allocates the whole pool for each KV tensor as a
single pinned tensor (torch.zeros(..., pin_memory=True)). On Intel XPU,
Level Zero refuses a zeMemAllocHost larger than the device's
maxMemAllocSize, which is about the card's local memory (between 30 and
31 GiB on a 32 GB Arc Pro B70, measured). A 128 GiB pool on TP4 asks for
32 GiB per rank:

- torch 2.13 does not check the result of sycl::aligned_alloc_host, returns
  a tensor with a null data pointer, and the zero-fill then writes from
  address 0: every rank segfaults at boot.
- torch 2.14 raises "Failed to allocate ... of pinned host memory."

The only workaround so far was a compute-runtime debug key
(NEOReadDebugKeys=1 AllowUnrestrictedSize=1), which also relaxes the size
check for device allocations.

This change keeps one tensor whenever it fits, and otherwise stores the
pool as equal row-aligned chunks:

- _max_pinned_host_alloc_bytes() reads the per-allocation limit where the
  platform reports it (torch.xpu device properties' max_mem_alloc_size;
  None on CUDA, where pinned host memory has no such cap). torch 2.13 and
  2.14 do not expose it yet, so on XPU a refused allocation (null pointer or
  RuntimeError) falls back to halving the chunk size until it fits.
- The chunk count is the smallest power of two for which each chunk fits,
  so chunks line up with the caching host allocator's power-of-two
  rounding (32 GiB -> 2 x 16 GiB, nothing wasted).
- ChunkedHostTensor maps a block id to (chunk, row) and exposes the tensor
  attributes the handler checks, but no data_ptr(), so code that assumes
  one base pointer fails loudly. compute_sub_block_ptrs resolves each
  block through its chunk; a row never straddles chunks, so every copy
  descriptor stays inside one allocation. Both the direct and the
  canonical descriptor fills go through it.
- A pinned allocation that returns a null pointer now raises
  PinnedHostAllocationError instead of being filled.
- One log line states the split.

When the pool fits (every CUDA/ROCm run, and XPU pools below the limit)
the result is one zeroed tensor as before; the allocation is now
torch.empty + a null check + zero_() instead of torch.zeros.

Diagnosis: a boot segfault of a 128 GiB offload pool on 4x
B70 was traced to this allocation from the kernel fault addresses (data
writes below 2^35 from the fill loop) and the torch 2.13
CachingHostAllocator source, then confirmed with a single-allocation
probe (torch 2.13 and 2.14, compute-runtime 26.35): 30 GiB pinned OK,
31 GiB refused (null on 2.13, RuntimeError on 2.14), 32/33 GiB OK with
AllowUnrestrictedSize, and torch.xpu device properties carry no
max_mem_alloc_size.

Tests: tests/v1/kv_offload/cpu/test_chunked_host_pool.py (CPU): split shape
at a forced small limit, block -> (chunk, row) pointers for 1 and 3 blocks
per chunk with skips, store and load round trips through the handler's
descriptor fill, the null-pointer guard, and the unreported-limit fallback.
The offload suites fail the same set before and after.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
